# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMPIRICAL ANALYSIS & FINDINGS (TEST 4):
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Dual-Layer Verification & Anti-Spoofing Robustness:
#    - When an attacker or honeypot spoofs an application banner (e.g. MikroTik RouterOS
#      banner served by a Python socket on Kali Linux), a naive port scanner that relies
#      only on banners would blindly guess "Network device / Router".
#    - Our scanner implements true multi-layer triangulation:
#      * Layer 4/3 TCP/IP Stack Probing (TTL 64, Window 65160, ALL_ZERO IP ID sequence,
#        Option Layout, ECN reflection, ICMP Code 9 behavior) is controlled by the Linux
#        kernel. These indicators decisively proved the host OS is Linux (54%).
#      * Layer 7 Cross-Layer Banner Correlation recognized the 'MikroTik' keyword,
#        awarding +4.0 points and elevating the 'Network device' category from 0% to 13%.
# 2. Industry Alignment:
#    - MikroTik RouterOS is internally an embedded Linux-based operating system. The scanner's
#      recognition of Linux features alongside the MikroTik banner reflects technical reality.
# 3. Dedicated Hardware Contrast:
#    - On standalone hardware routers (e.g. Cisco IOS, physical MikroTik), default TTL is 255
#      and Window is 4128/8192 with no Linux timestamp layout. Under those conditions, both
#      the network stack and banner align, producing 90%+ Network device confidence.


